## Objective

This PR makes it easier for developers to reflect the progress of async actions in the UI for both forms and standalone buttons (such as `Refresh` buttons). Error handling is also implemented for convenience.

This is meant to replace the older `appApiAction` directive, modernize it to use observables and add it to the CL.

## Usage example for standalone buttons

### Standard method
```html
<button type="button" [loading]="loading" (click)="refresh()">Refresh</button>
```
```ts
class Component {
  loading = false;
  
  constructor(private validationService: ValidationService) {}
  
  async refresh() {
    this.loading = true;
    
    try {
      // [... refresh logic ...]
    } catch (err) {
      this.validationService.showError(err);
    } finally {
      this.loading = false;
    }
  }
}
```


### (New in this PR) `bitAction` method
```html
<button type="button" [bitAction]="refresh">Refresh</button>
```
```ts
class Component {  
  refresh = async () => {
      // [... refresh logic ...]
  }
}
```

## Usage example in forms

### Standard method
```html
<form [formGroup]="formGroup" (ngSubmit)="submit()">
  <button type="submit" bitButton [loading]="loading">
    {{ "submit" | i18n }}
  </button>
</form>
```
```ts
class Component {
  loading = false;
  formGroup = this.formBuilder.group({...})
  
  constructor(private validationService: ValidationService) {}
  
  async submit() {
    if (this.formGroup.invalid) {
      return;
    }
  
    this.loading = true;
    try {
      // [... submit logic ...]
    } catch (err) {
      this.validationService.showError(err);
    } finally {
      this.loading = false;
    }
  }
}
```

### `appApiAction` method
```html
<form #form (ngSubmit)="submit()" [appApiAction]="formPromise">
  <button type="submit" bitButton [loading]="form.loading">
    {{ "submit" | i18n }}
  </button>
</form>
```

```ts
class Component {
  formPromise: Promise<unknown>;
  formGroup = this.formBuilder.group({...})
  
  async submit() {
    if (this.formGroup.invalid) {
      return;
    }
   
    this.formPromise = this.doSubmit();
    await this.formPromise;
  }
  
  private async doSubmit() {
    // [... submit logic ...]
  }
}
```

### (New in this PR) `bitSubmit` method
```html
<form [formGroup]="formGroup" [bitSubmit]="submit">
  <button type="submit" bitFormButton>
    {{ "submit" | i18n }}
  </button>
</form>
```

```ts
class Component {
  formGroup = this.formBuilder.group({...})
  
  //note: submit can also return Observable instead of Promise
  submit: async () => {
    if (this.formGroup.invalid) {
      return;
    }
    
    // [... submit logic ...]
  }
}
```

## Code changes

- **bit-action.directive:**  New directive that allow a single button to perform async actions on click and reflect their progress in the UI.

- **bit-submit.directive:** New directive that is similar to the directive above. The difference is that it attaches to a `form` tag with `[formGroup]` directive and performs the async action on submit instead of click. Submit buttons are disabled and show loading animation. The form and regular buttons are also disabled.
   A bit more detailed explanation: 
   The directive attaches to a form, extracts the `FormGroupDirective` and hooks into `ngSubmit`. When the user submits, `BitSubmitDirective` picks up the action and triggers the developer-supplied async submit handler. The status of the async execution is exposed using the public `loading$` observable field.
   Note: If the submit handler returns an `Observable` it will automatically be unsubscribed and aborted if the UI is destroyed.
   
- **form-button.directive:** A layer in-between `bitButton` or `biticonButton` and `bitSubmit` or `bitAction`. It subscribes to `BitSubmitDirective.loading$` and sets `BitButtonDirective.loading` accordingly. This results in the button automatically disabling and showing loading state when the async submit handler is running. On regular buttons it makes sure the buttons are disabled while submitting. If regular buttons have a `bitAction` it makes sure the form is disabled when the action is performed.

